==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|set theory|order theory|"on" a set A}} A [[subset]] of the [[Cartesian product]] ''A''×''A'' (the set of [[ordered pair]]s (''a'', ''b'') of [[element]]s of ''A'').
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|author=w:George Grätzer|title=General Lattice Theory|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=OdPQs1wx8ZIC&pg=PA1&dq=%22binary+relation%22%7C%22binary+relations%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiXs8KD2drbAhXIx7wKHexSAlUQ6AEI4wEwJQ#v=onepage&q=%22binary%20relation%22%7C%22binary%20relations%22&f=false|page=1|publisher=Academic Press
|passage=A ''partially ordered set'' <math>\langle A,\varrho\rangle</math> consists of a nonvoid set <math>A</math> and a '''binary relation''' <math>\varrho</math> on <math>A</math>, such that <math>\varrho</math> satisfies properties (P1)-(P3).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=James C. Moore|title=Mathematical Methods for Economic Theory 1|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=akdEl1R0K6sC&pg=PA24&dq=%22binary+relation%22%7C%22binary+relations%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiXs8KD2drbAhXIx7wKHexSAlUQ6AEIhAEwEw#v=onepage&q=%22binary%20relation%22%7C%22binary%20relations%22&f=false|page=24|publisher=Springer
|passage='''1.30. Corollary.''' ''If P is a '''binary relation''' which is asymmetric and negatively transitive, then P is also transitive.''<br>It should be noted that a '''binary relation''' may be irreflexive and negatively transitive without being transitive; as an example, consider the standard inequality relation (≠).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=T. S. Blyth|title=Lattices and Ordered Algebraic Structures|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=jQYwCI__1mgC&pg=PA1&dq=%22binary+relation%22%7C%22binary+relations%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwiXs8KD2drbAhXIx7wKHexSAlUQ6AEIswIwNA#v=onepage&q=%22binary%20relation%22%7C%22binary%20relations%22&f=false|page=1|publisher=Springer
|passage='''Definition''' If ''E'' is a non-empty set then by an '''order''' on ''E'' we mean a '''binary relation''' on ''E'' that is reflexive, anti-symmetric, and transitive.}}
# {{lb|en|set theory|order theory|"on" or "between" sets A and B}} A [[subset]] of the [[Cartesian product]] ''A''×''B''.

====Usage notes====
*If <math>R</math> is a relation between <math>A</math> and <math>B</math>, then <math>A</math> is called the ''[[domain]]'' of the relation, and <math>B</math> is called the ''[[codomain]]'' of the relation.
*For a binary relation <math>R</math>, the notation <math>a R b</math> signifies that <math>(a,b) \in R</math>, and one may say that <math>a</math> is in binary relation <math>R</math> to <math>b</math>. 

